#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "a6d910ae-c6db-51d2-93ba-f6c823c0a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6179" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z= x^2 + xy$ at the point $(1,2, 3).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2) & =2(1)+2=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) \\\\ z-3 & =4(x-1)+1(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =4x+y-6 \\\\ -4x-y+z+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+y-z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "4eb4806c-3d5b-5ed2-87a1-d1ed50c267e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6182" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={y^2}+ {xy}$  is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ec/ec631b90dd9a037263e163bc32ebd982de5f60ee813622ae7b4f1a6b153fe856.svg) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1a3960-c2c4-57e7-8fd1-daf5d98bf052" :question/math-academy-id "e-6185" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x^2+y^2+z^2-2xyz$ at the point $(1,-1,1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0,w_0)$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw - w_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (z-z_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(x_0,y_0,z_0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2x-2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2y-2xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2z-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z) = (1,-1,1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)-2(-1)(1)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =2(-1)-2(1)(1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)-2(1)(-1)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-5 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y+1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-5 & =4(x-1)-4(y+1)+4(z-1) \\\\ w-5 & =4x-4y+4z-12 \\\\ -4x+4y-4z+w+7 & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+4z-w-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d75fcdf-fb78-57bb-b854-212e11099d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x-18y-z-20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383113} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6316d6-6422-54c0-86cd-72086b4081b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+18y+z-16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383114} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0e01e1-4c28-56f2-b144-a20ed4da587d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383115} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a54cb-8009-5f37-84a1-8f7928a1dae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3y+z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383116} {:answer/id #uuid "f208b59b-1fb8-53fd-a77a-32b65a79d311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-9y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d75fcdf-fb78-57bb-b854-212e11099d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x-18y-z-20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383113} :answer-field/id #uuid "facf18b7-00ac-5d4c-a780-571ce69d518f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58da2e0b-eb6e-5d08-a4d3-1469fcdda740" :question/math-academy-id "q-129435" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7$ at the point $(1,-1,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7) \\\\  & =8x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7) \\\\  & =18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1) & =8(1)=8 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1) & =18(-1)=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-6 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1)+(y-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1) \\\\ z-6 & =8(x-1)-18(y+1) \\\\ z-6 & =8x-18y-26 \\\\ 8x-18y-z-20 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150b29fd-0fda-563c-b759-e7ee2595528f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263195} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dbbc6f-6c1c-50bf-acbd-aa66f4569d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264450} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8631e3-9694-5175-9bf4-f7367575916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264791} {:answer/id #uuid "e11e1d12-29ed-5223-87de-a3ca1b64e0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264957} {:answer/id #uuid "f474ceab-0542-5f8a-ad55-4bc6e07c5e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150b29fd-0fda-563c-b759-e7ee2595528f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263195} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78dad6-0647-5819-b4c6-f3f403293c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9c6a200-53f4-5ee2-ac37-189c11ad9a9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129439" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3$ at the point $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) & =-4(1)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) \\\\ z-1 & =0x-4(y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =-4y+4 \\\\ 4y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db81038-14ab-5c5a-a5ad-97fdab24991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-y-8z+4w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263814} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea602ac-5be9-5c1c-bf26-9a06350f1d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-y-z+2w+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263959} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9bc1-b39a-51f9-913c-1d411b519d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-z-w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264460} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d74fea-1922-5462-9deb-c74cb5dc8d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-2z+w-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264576} {:answer/id #uuid "cba1d5ec-fac2-58d8-8154-90dcb2d67e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2y+z+w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5db81038-14ab-5c5a-a5ad-97fdab24991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-y-8z+4w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263814} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8011146-a401-58f3-97b3-4121fbdc7250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152fa2a1-ccb2-5b4a-abda-500e8681371b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129483" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\sqrt{y}-x$ at the point $(-1,4,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,4,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-3 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1)+(y-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) \\\\ w-3 & =-(x+1)+\\frac{1}{4}(y-4)+2(z-1) \\\\ w-3 & =-x+\\frac{1}{4}y+2z-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{4}y-2z+w+1 & =0 \\\\ 4x-y-8z+4w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34e35c54-54e0-5524-839e-eeaca1d4f5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,-2)$ and $(1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263464} {:answer/id #uuid "5c42a3fa-67df-5c3c-a81c-bb141d4d3e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263795} {:answer/id #uuid "86442d7a-dc52-5cb3-bca6-f3803dcb8c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264158} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fc004f-644b-5bcd-8c60-1ccf1aab45bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264381} {:answer/id #uuid "b5acb9d4-7d71-5ab0-85e7-6cd20512909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86442d7a-dc52-5cb3-bca6-f3803dcb8c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264158} :answer-field/id #uuid "485b9650-0321-56bc-982a-914d5669cbdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "614f0c59-2007-5d79-93a8-aaee2f0fc248" :question/math-academy-id "q-129554" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{3}+xy$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y &=0 \\\\ x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4831fde7-328b-5f95-8b4f-bd555404f79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263624} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9d3b86-7aec-5970-92c9-47d3d544786f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263789} {:answer/id #uuid "94d3b3cb-73a3-5f94-abc9-28adc6612ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264274} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264345} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e3749-cf7d-5ffc-ac93-9256ee36c08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264345} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b8a09a-7de4-5c87-b2a5-460a354244c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129557" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6cx &=4 \\\\ 2cy &=-2 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8deb005a-21c9-5869-a504-2b64c9fbb5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex + ey - ez - w = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127154} {:answer/id #uuid "9b17fcd2-b57f-5441-93a0-9f52c4d33e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex + ey + ez - w = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127155} {:answer/id #uuid "f63c232e-c823-5881-bd5c-69c0b0a25123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - z - w = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127156} {:answer/id #uuid "fa553a04-aca2-53ad-984d-459830ccfc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y - z - w = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127157} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1809d3-628c-5d7f-ac90-9e2443cf5232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex - ey - ez - w = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127158}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3c30558f-4c15-5b6d-ba15-6c67ecb30a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1f92c54-f156-5d20-a5fc-d8893cc3aada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex+ey-ez-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385397} {:answer/id #uuid "95631a5f-2220-508e-87a2-4dfcf8b89a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385398} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1abf36-7231-5484-8476-690dda16f242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex-ey-ez-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385399} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dabfcd-21e1-5714-914e-00d8d59f8639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex+ey+ez-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385400} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb06889-55cc-555b-8cd1-fd3c52a2f8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f92c54-f156-5d20-a5fc-d8893cc3aada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ex+ey-ez-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385397} :answer-field/id #uuid "d537076e-5b52-598a-ab67-0819d7af7245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf18ba79-26dd-5213-bc52-5eab8640b606" :question/math-academy-id "q-37604" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z}$ at the point $(1,1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})=-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1}=e \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1}=e \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =-{e}^{1}=-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-e & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w-e & =e(x-1)+e(y-1)-e(z-1) \\\\ w-e & =ex-e+ey-e-ez+e \\\\ -ex-ey+ez+w & =0 \\\\ ex+ey-ez-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53035ba-54ff-5719-a43f-a5b375e24b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x + 3y - 5z - 8 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127160} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6049fe-e2a3-5678-a6ef-41464f0529b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - 7y + 5z + 8 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127161} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0fe123-f9b0-5508-a763-c74d7b2ed42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + 7y - 5z - 8 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127162} {:answer/id #uuid "24126c2d-942e-51a0-85a9-e86fbdbf7bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x - 3y - 8z - 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127163} {:answer/id #uuid "46c23c3f-cd7a-5788-b467-8c694595e6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x + 2y - 5z + 8 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127164}] :answer-field/id #uuid "32a72fc2-fa65-5357-8c3e-47084d07ba43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127159}] :question/id #uuid "479c24f7-c298-5da5-afa2-e5f0e7c301cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-37721" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = \\sqrt{x^{2} + 2y^{3}} - y$ at the point $(3, 2, 3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a95c8a6-3c01-5a9e-921c-caa2b833418a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321353} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa273c2-815f-513d-b54f-c29d6f572500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321899} {:answer/id #uuid "96a14160-ecda-5210-90d5-5bc2eeb9acdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-6)$, $(1,0,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322349} {:answer/id #uuid "a656e67e-614a-5d4e-9b20-18e1bc0e66ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,-6)$, $(1,0,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322448} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4d81bd-57b9-5ec9-8c17-620b08113a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa273c2-815f-513d-b54f-c29d6f572500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321899} :answer-field/id #uuid "682cef21-fac3-5749-a2b9-4a5ac1928c11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a17d4f6d-c761-5e5d-8256-475aa2ea42b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-37990" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6$  is the tangent plane \nhorizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =8xy+{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨8xy+{y}^{3},\\,4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy+{y}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$ or ${y}^{2}+8x=0.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(0)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\n- If ${y}^{2}=-8x$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(-8x)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ back into ${y}^{2}=-8x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-8(0)\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, in both cases, we have $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Finally, substituting these values into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=4(0)^{2}(0)+(0)(0)^{3}-6=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b102e1c7-5fa3-51db-bcc8-e83a72b1e4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127232} {:answer/id #uuid "b89cc3a1-82e1-53f2-980b-4bed8ff81fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 1, \\ln 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127233} {:answer/id #uuid "fe339e36-cccf-5997-a7f6-bb02afa8fc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 1, \\ln 3)$ and $(-1,-1, \\ln 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127234} {:answer/id #uuid "35d4182d-b5d3-57bf-9d7a-3a2560f1433c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 0, \\ln 2)$ and $(0, 1, \\ln 2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127235} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6f9152-70e9-5b61-9099-02b29a3686ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127236}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3a4998-2b09-5d83-8e03-e1912b05cd88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1e9f0a-90a7-54b0-9e36-e98cea0ded22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ and $(0,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385403} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fb4a9b-9fc1-5e80-a02b-6488af643ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385404} {:answer/id #uuid "669516e8-737c-5944-9a7c-29afe955b2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385405} {:answer/id #uuid "eb172134-ac0b-586a-bb93-2e5c9434a22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ and $(-1,-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385406} {:answer/id #uuid "598fc98c-c733-57f6-9433-21ab70e0af03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669516e8-737c-5944-9a7c-29afe955b2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385405} :answer-field/id #uuid "a712c3f4-1e32-50a0-8746-5155fe0c7f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f0c2107-2927-516f-9a87-f35b56581e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37993" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07891df9-c86b-5d2c-934e-1198e15effab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339192} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdaaab6-7164-5dff-b40c-6e4d3f7548e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339662} {:answer/id #uuid "741509e6-6fbe-5fe9-a5ba-a086aedabd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340157} {:answer/id #uuid "77b13d4c-45e2-5a1c-8d59-a148a5c50612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340195} {:answer/id #uuid "adbb5319-2de4-5c33-84a5-55847de001a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "741509e6-6fbe-5fe9-a5ba-a086aedabd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340157} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a8ebe0-d46c-59a8-8a29-0dc77165a09a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f18efd9d-f823-53b1-9b52-0bc881b7962b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73842" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1$ at the point $(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1) \\\\ z-1 & =1\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =x+y-2 \\\\ -x-y+z+1 & =0 \\\\ x+y-z-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fcc3f3-ddff-52b4-b6ee-b406a0ffec98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y-z+w+\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340008} {:answer/id #uuid "93a5ca28-3798-5c54-9d92-aa2d41eb6d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y+z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340453} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8794dd-7491-5377-b367-530775903444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z-w+3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340570} {:answer/id #uuid "f6251148-2e5e-526a-995c-c61130894b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y-z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341302} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341343} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31f7eac-9e1f-5aaa-97e4-e132312fc936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de6bd964-56b9-511c-9d67-ed8d06732a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73851" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a018c8-a299-5802-ab99-b716146f99d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2z+w-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339277} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c54b5e-1ed7-5942-913e-45a6ea5c4afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2z+w+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340776} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85ee97-8a47-5ab6-b016-ccb940986f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2y+2z+w-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340813} {:answer/id #uuid "dc25e3d9-36ed-50b2-9070-e977db192aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2z-w-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341085} {:answer/id #uuid "ea466c85-8697-51aa-96cb-e866210b3f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y+2z+w-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a018c8-a299-5802-ab99-b716146f99d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2z+w-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339277} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cef762e-0604-5f20-834f-3818ac0664e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4142a5f1-3da0-59f6-9d01-2beeae09fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73853" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})=2xyz+{y}^{2}z+y{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})={x}^{2}z+2xyz+x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+2xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}(1)+(-1)(1)^{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =(1)^{2}(1)+2(1)(-1)(1)+(1)(1)^{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =(1)^{2}(-1)+(1)(-1)^{2}+2(1)(-1)(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-(-1) & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) \\\\ w+1 & =-2(x-1)+0\\cdot (y+1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w+1 & =-2x-2z+4 \\\\ 2x+2z+w-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22adf989-5e2a-597b-819b-21a19def1126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385594} {:answer/id #uuid "11941d78-3eb3-50c3-b666-e730845aaa3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385595} {:answer/id #uuid "aa179fe0-208b-5917-b108-3ef3511520db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385596} {:answer/id #uuid "2d827ff9-9c8d-5c4d-b211-cd413a42b353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385597} {:answer/id #uuid "d88c04db-3d43-558a-9cc6-1dc2e4ad785c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,2)$ and $(1,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa179fe0-208b-5917-b108-3ef3511520db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385596} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc46821c-d90f-57c3-87fa-76db78d17b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4e60eff-c04e-592a-8b2a-1cfa75b26598" :question/math-academy-id "q-73863" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-4,0,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2x+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x+2,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-4,0,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2x+2,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩=⟨-4,0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c(2x+2) &=-4 \\\\ c(2y+2) &=0 \\\\ -c &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-(2x+2)=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-(2y+2)=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(1)^{2}+2(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(1,-1,2).$"}]}
